Certainly! Here's a JavaScript program that demonstrates the use of advanced features such as promises, async/await, destructuring, and the spread operator in a complex manner:

```javascript
class DataFetcher {
  constructor(urls) {
    this.urls = urls;
  }

  async fetchData() {
    const fetchPromises = this.urls.map(async (url) => {
      const response = await fetch(url);
      if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`Failed to fetch ${url}`);
      return await response.json();
    });

    try {
      return await Promise.all(fetchPromises);
    } catch (error) {
      console.error(error);
    }
  }
}

class DataProcessor {
  static process(data) {
    return data.map(item => {
      const { id, name, ...rest } = item;
      return { id, name, details: { ...rest } };
    });
  }
}

(async () => {
  const urls = [
    'https: 
    'https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/users/2',
  ];

  const fetcher = new DataFetcher(urls);
  const rawData = await fetcher.fetchData();
  const processedData = DataProcessor.process(rawData);

  console.log(processedData);
})();
```

This code defines a `DataFetcher` class that handles asynchronous data fetching from provided URLs using `async/await`. It then processes the data by leveraging ES6 destructuring and the spread operator within the `DataProcessor` class to transform the data into a desired format. Finally, it executes the data fetching and processing using an IIFE (Immediately Invoked Function Expression).